### Linked In Learning

Video 3: ***Clarifying Questions*** 

*"Remember, what's obvious to you might not be obvious to everyone else." *

I like this statement because I feel I'm especially aware of this. My way of thinking tends to group me in the cognitive minority most everywhere I go. I'm keen to breakdown ideas and extract the real meaning. Sometimes people are highly intentional and inventive with their language. Other times they piggy-bag off of decades if not centuries of cultural infusion into the language they speak and the phrases they use without processing what they're actually referencing to express their idea to someone else. I do hear what people are saying but I'm also listening to the cultural undertone and using that to scrape additional context and perspective as well.

:::





## Questions

1. **Crossroads:** Does the 50% discount promotion at BYU–Idaho Crossroads increase total daily revenue compared to non-discount days due to increased purchase volume?

2. **Pass/Fail:** Do less-experienced professors have higher student failure rates than more tenured<span style="color: red;">\*</span> professors, and does this relationship vary across 100–400 level courses? <span style="color: red;">\*</span><span style="color: #ccb;">(Should I say tenured?)</span>

<!-- Question about word choice in question 2 -->

3. **Salary:** Do average time-to-graduation (number of semesters) differ by major at BYU–Idaho, and is longer time-to-degree associated with higher post-graduation salaries? (*Note: Salaries may need to be measured in time ranges of 5, 10, and 15 years*).

4. **Temperature:** Is average daily temperature during Fall, Winter, and Spring semesters associated with differences in average final course grades at BYU–Idaho over time (year-over-year)? (*Note: This is data-heavy and confounded: Housing, Stress, Course difficulty, Instructor, COVID years, etc...*)





